Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Molten Glass Bites the Concrete
- Story & Context: When Mireille Baptiste hotwired thirty imported heat lamps into the high-voltage busbar of the decommissioned accelerator, the tunnel filled with the sharp stench of scorched copper and rotting mango peels. The resulting power surge blew the vacuum-seal gauges and showered the marimba in incandescent glass shards, but Paul simply locked into a frantic cowbell rhythm while the residual beamline magnets generated a chest-crushing sub-drone. The terrified crowd erupted into a leaping frenzy, screaming Mireille's distorted megaphone refrain, "Shake the lead until the sugar flies!" until security bolts rattled in their frames.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: High-energy concert album artwork capturing a curved subterranean concrete ring illuminated by shattering incandescent glass and streaming yellow sunlight lamps, with droplets of mango juice and airborne sweat frozen in mid-air against dark industrial conduit pipes, cinematic and kinetic.

Track 2: "Who Cracked the Rose Window?"
- Story & Context: Crouched inside Target Chamber Bravo with grease-stained knuckles and cold sweat dripping into his hibiscus tea, Julien Vane confessed to triggering the 3 AM blackout that almost got the venue raided. Attempting to soften the facility's harsh sodium glare, he had hoisted a salvaged church window pane onto an overhead gantry crane, only for the rusty chain to snap and scatter stained-light fragments across the amplifier stacks. Over a shivering solitary hoover synth hovering above the floor's eternal low hum, Julien wept into his vocoder and sang the heartbroken hook, "I only wanted daylight in the dark!" before the full rhythm section detonated back to life.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Melancholic yet radiant visual of multicolored stained-glass shards scattered across an oily industrial metal grating, illuminated from below by an intense radioactive magenta glow, cast in moody volumetric tunnel dust and cinematic lens flares.

Track 3: Sector Nine Beam Dump
- Story & Context: When municipal inspectors finally broke through the reinforced blast doors of Sector Nine at noon, they found no squatters, only hundreds of worn rubber plimsolls stuck to the floor by dried guava syrup and lime pulp. The raid turned into a bewildered vigil when the officers realized the PA system had been hard-soldered into the particle deceleration coils, endlessly circulating a warm, sunny 40-Hz hum that vibrated the fillings in their teeth. Smeared across the lead shielding in pink body paint was the scene's ultimate mythic farewell, "The circle never turns you home!" reverberating as the track dissolved into infinite steelpan delay.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Post-rave morning aftermath scene inside an abandoned circular particle physics bay, showing abandoned colorful instruments, scattered citrus rinds, and glowing luminescent graffiti slogans on lead shielding, bathed in rays of real sunlight breaking through an open blast hatch.

